<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter3_function-overloading">
  <title>Function Overloading</title>

  <p>
    A program often needs to perform the same general task with different
    kinds of data. <term>Function overloading</term> allows several functions
    to use the same name when their parameter lists are different. The
    compiler selects the function whose parameters best match the arguments
    in a particular call.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a program can calculate a total for either two integers or
    two floating-point values:
  </p>

  <program xml:id="program_chapter3_function_overloading_basic"
           language="cpp" interactive="yes">
    <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int calculate_total(int first_value, int second_value) {
    return first_value + second_value;
}

double calculate_total(double first_value, double second_value) {
    return first_value + second_value;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << calculate_total(3, 4) << '\n';
    std::cout << calculate_total(2.5, 1.5) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
    ]]></code>
  </program>

  <p>
    Both functions are named <c>calculate_total</c>, but the first accepts two
    <c>int</c> parameters and the second accepts two <c>double</c> parameters.
    The call <c>calculate_total(3, 4)</c> selects the integer version, while
    <c>calculate_total(2.5, 1.5)</c> selects the floating-point version.
  </p>

  <subsection xml:id="chapter3_function-overloading_parameter-lists">
    <title>What Makes an Overload Different?</title>

    <p>
      The part of a function declaration that distinguishes overloads is its
      <term>parameter list</term>: the number, types, and order of the
      parameters. Parameter names do not count as differences.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table_chapter3_function-overloading_valid-differences">
      <title>Differences between overloaded functions</title>
      <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
        <col width="30%"/>
        <col width="16%"/>
        <col width="54%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Difference</cell>
          <cell>Valid?</cell>
          <cell>Explanation</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Number of parameters</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell><c>report_value(int)</c> and <c>report_value(int, int)</c> are different.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Parameter types</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell><c>report_value(int)</c> and <c>report_value(double)</c> are different.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Parameter order</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell><c>set_position(int, double)</c> and <c>set_position(double, int)</c> are different.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Parameter names</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>Names describe parameters but do not change a function's parameter list.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Return type only</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>The compiler cannot select a function using only the return type.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      These declarations are valid overloads because their parameter lists
      contain different types:
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_chapter3_function_overloading_parameter_types"
             language="cpp">
      <code><![CDATA[
void report_value(int value);
void report_value(double value);
void report_value(const std::string& value);
      ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
      The following declarations are not valid overloads. They differ only in
      return type:
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_chapter3_function_overloading_return_type_only"
             language="cpp">
      <code><![CDATA[
// Invalid: return type alone cannot distinguish overloads.
int get_score();
double get_score();
      ]]></code>
    </program>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="chapter3_function-overloading_selection">
    <title>How the Compiler Selects an Overload</title>

    <p>
      When the compiler sees a function call, it compares the argument types
      with the parameter types of the available overloads. An exact match is
      preferred. For example, an <c>int</c> argument selects an overload with
      an <c>int</c> parameter when that overload is available.
    </p>

    <p>
      A call can also use a standard conversion when no exact match is
      available. However, relying on several possible conversions can make a
      call ambiguous. An ambiguous call is one for which the compiler cannot
      choose one best overload, so the program does not compile.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_chapter3_function_overloading_conversion"
             language="cpp">
      <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

void report_value(long value) {
    std::cout << "long: " << value << '\n';
}

void report_value(double value) {
    std::cout << "double: " << value << '\n';
}

int main() {
    // report_value(4);  // Ambiguous: int can convert to long or double.
    report_value(4L);    // Exact match for long.
    report_value(4.0);   // Exact match for double.

    return 0;
}
      ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Default arguments require similar care. If two overloads can accept the
      same call after default arguments are applied, the call may be
      ambiguous:
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_chapter3_function_overloading_default_argument"
             language="cpp">
      <code><![CDATA[
void display_value(int value);
void display_value(int value, int width = 10);

// display_value(5);  // Ambiguous: both declarations can accept this call.
      ]]></code>
    </program>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="chapter3_function-overloading_design-guidance">
    <title>Designing an Overload Set</title>

    <p>
      Overloads should represent the same general operation for different
      kinds or amounts of data. A reader should be able to predict that
      functions with the same name perform closely related tasks.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use one name when the operation has the same meaning for different
          parameter types.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Keep overloads simple enough that a call has one obvious match.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Avoid overloads that depend on surprising implicit conversions.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Do not create overloads that differ only by return type or parameter
          names.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Constructor overloading, introduced earlier in this book, follows the
      same central idea: one class can provide several constructors with
      different parameter lists. Function overloading applies that idea to
      ordinary named functions.
    </p>

    <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter3_function_overloading"
                 iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aint%20calculate_total%28int%20first_value%2C%20int%20second_value%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20return%20first_value%20%2B%20second_value%3B%0A%7D%0A%0Adouble%20calculate_total%28double%20first_value%2C%20double%20second_value%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20return%20first_value%20%2B%20second_value%3B%0A%7D%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20calculate_total%283%2C%204%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20calculate_total%282.5%2C%201.5%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=360&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;curInstr=1&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                 width="100%"
                 aspect="16:10">
      <description>
        <p>
          C++ Tutor visualization showing two overloaded
          <c>calculate_total</c> functions and the calls that select them.
        </p>
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="chapter3_function-overloading_summary">
    <title>Summary</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Function overloading gives related functions the same name.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Overloaded functions must have different parameter lists.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Parameter count, parameter types, and parameter order can distinguish
          overloads.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Parameter names and return types alone cannot distinguish overloads.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The compiler selects the best matching overload, but conversions and
          default arguments can create ambiguity.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Good overloads represent one clear operation for different forms of
          data.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <exercise label="chapter3_function_overloading_1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which pair of declarations is a valid pair of overloaded functions?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><c>void report_value(int value);</c> and <c>void report_value(double value);</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Correct. The parameter types are different.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><c>int get_value();</c> and <c>double get_value();</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Return type alone cannot distinguish overloaded functions.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><c>void print(int value);</c> and <c>void print(int number);</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>The parameter names are different, but both functions have the same parameter list.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><c>double calculate(double value);</c> and <c>double calculate(double amount);</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>The parameter names do not distinguish the functions.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="chapter3_function_overloading_2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which call selects the overload with two <c>double</c> parameters?
      </p>
      <program language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
int calculate_total(int first_value, int second_value);
double calculate_total(double first_value, double second_value);
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><c>calculate_total(3, 4)</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Both arguments are <c>int</c> values, so the integer overload is an exact match.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><c>calculate_total(3.0, 4.0)</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Correct. Both arguments are <c>double</c> values.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><c>calculate_total(3L, 4L)</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>These arguments are <c>long</c> values, not exact <c>double</c> values.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><c>calculate_total(3, 4.0)</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>This call has mixed argument types and does not provide two exact <c>double</c> arguments.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="chapter3_function_overloading_3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why is the following pair invalid?
      </p>
      <program language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
int get_score();
double get_score();
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Both functions have the same return type.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>The return types are different, not the same.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>The parameter lists are identical, and return type alone cannot overload a function.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Correct. A call cannot select between these functions using only its return context.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>A function cannot return a numeric value.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>C++ functions can return numeric values. The problem is the identical parameter list.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Function names must always be different.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Overloaded functions intentionally share a name when their parameter lists differ.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="chapter3_function_overloading_4"
            adaptive="yes"
            indentation="show">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Arrange the blocks to define two overloads of
        <c>calculate_total</c> and call both versions from <c>main</c>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
      <block order="1"><cline><![CDATA[#include <iostream>]]></cline></block>
      <block order="2"><cline><![CDATA[int calculate_total(int first_value, int second_value) {]]></cline></block>
      <block order="3"><cline><![CDATA[    return first_value + second_value;]]></cline><cline>}</cline></block>
      <block order="4"><cline><![CDATA[double calculate_total(double first_value, double second_value) {]]></cline></block>
      <block order="5"><cline><![CDATA[    return first_value + second_value;]]></cline><cline>}</cline></block>
      <block order="6"><cline>int main() {</cline></block>
      <block order="7"><cline><![CDATA[    std::cout << calculate_total(3, 4) << '\n';]]></cline></block>
      <block order="8"><cline><![CDATA[    std::cout << calculate_total(2.5, 1.5) << '\n';]]></cline></block>
      <block order="9"><cline>    return 0;</cline><cline>}</cline></block>
    </blocks>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="chapter3_function_overloading_5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the main problem with the following call?
      </p>
      <program language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
void report_value(long value);
void report_value(double value);

report_value(4);
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The function name is too long.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Function names can contain multiple words when the naming convention permits them.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The argument must be a string.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>The argument is a valid numeric value, but it can convert in more than one way here.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>The call is ambiguous because an <c>int</c> can convert to either <c>long</c> or <c>double</c>.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Correct. Neither conversion is a uniquely best match.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Overloaded functions cannot have numeric parameters.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Numeric parameter types are commonly used in overloads.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
</section>
